{
  "unit": {
    "id": "4",
    "title": "Giving orders",
    "blurb": "Commands, vocative names, and nōlī…",
    "intro": "A Roman parent, teacher or officer speaks in commands. To one person, the command is the verb's stem: _bibe!_ (drink!), _lege!_ (read!), _venī!_ (come!). To several people add -te: _bibite!_, _legite!_, _venīte!_. To forbid, use _nōlī_ (to one) or _nōlīte_ (to several) plus the infinitive: _nōlī bibere!_ = don't drink! When you speak to someone by name, the name takes the vocative: _Mārcus_ becomes _Mārce_."
  },
